\section{Strategies}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"global"} (the classical approach): one threshold on the score for all
observations. Under class imbalance it tends to reject the minority
classes much more often than the majority class.
\item \code{"class_conditional"} (class-conditional coverage matching, Algorithm 1
of Sağlam et al., 2026): one threshold per \strong{predicted} class, the
\code{alpha}-quantile of the calibration scores of the observations predicted
as that class. Every class is therefore accepted at about the same rate.
A new observation is accepted when its score is at most the threshold of
its predicted class. This needs enough calibration observations in each
predicted class.
\item \code{"label"} (LABEL, Sadinle, Lei and Wasserman, 2019, split-conformal
